5. D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
Simple definition

High-level classes should depend on abstractions, not concrete classes.

This is extremely important in Spring Boot.

❌ Without DIP

Suppose:

public class UserService {

    private MySQLDatabase database = new MySQLDatabase();

    public void saveUser(User user) {
        database.save(user);
    }
}

UserService directly depends on:

MySQLDatabase

Now imagine we want PostgreSQL.

We have to modify UserService.

✅ With DIP

Create an interface:

public interface UserRepository {

    void save(User user);
}

MySQL:

@Repository
public class MySQLUserRepository implements UserRepository {

    @Override
    public void save(User user) {
        // save to MySQL
    }
}

Service:

@Service
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository repository;

    public UserService(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    public void saveUser(User user) {
        repository.save(user);
    }
}

Notice:

UserService
     ↓
UserRepository
     ↑
MySQLUserRepository

UserService doesn't care whether the database is:

MySQL
PostgreSQL
MongoDB
Oracle

It only knows:

UserRepository
Why Spring Boot is closely related to DIP

This is where Dependency Injection comes in.

Spring does this:

@Service
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository repository;

    public UserService(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Spring creates the dependency and injects it.

Instead of:

UserRepository repository = new MySQLUserRepository();

you say:

UserRepository repository;

and Spring provides the implementation.

This gives you:

Loose coupling
Easy testing
Easy replacement of implementations
Easier maintenance